## std.env — environment variable lookup.
##
## From docs/spec.md §6.4 (System Surfaces): getEnv returns `?str`
## (None if the variable is unset). The codegen router entry in
## src/codegen/builtins.zig maps `getEnv` (arity=1) to the `env_var`
## dispatch and genBuiltinCall's arm emits:
##
##   blk: {
##       var __env_0: ?[]const u8 = null;
##       if (std.posix.toPosixPath(<args[0]>)) |_z| {
##           if (std.posix.system.getenv(&_z)) |__s| {
##               __env_0 = std.mem.span(__s);
##           }
##       }
##       break :blk __env_0;
##   }
##
## `std.posix.toPosixPath(<args[0]>)` defensively copies the arg into a
## `[PATH_MAX - 1:0]u8` sentinel-terminated stack buffer; the success
## arm gives a `*[4095:0]u8` whose `@ptrCast` lands cleanly on the
## `[*:0]const u8` argument type of `std.posix.system.getenv`. The
## nested-`if`-pattern (vs an `orelse`/catch fallthrough) lets the error
## arm of toPosixPath silently degrade to `env_val = null` — matching
## the `?str` contract where unset / unavailable / too-long arg are
## uniform "None" from the caller's perspective.
##
## `std.posix.system.getenv` aliases to `std.c.getenv` when the build
## links libc (typical for user-facing binaries), and to the per-OS
## syscall layer (linux/windows/wasi/...) otherwise. It returns
## `?[*:0]u8` natively, which we bridge to zag's `?[]const u8` shape via
## `std.mem.span` — the non-null arm's `[*:0]u8` pointer coerces to a
## `[]const u8` slice via `mem.span`. The `?[]const u8` zag-side type
## follows the docs/11 borrowed-string-view contract: a non-null result
## is a sentinel-terminated slice to a borrowed, NOT-owned buffer (env
## strings live in the process env block, freed by the OS at exit).
## Users wanting heap ownership must `copy` or `String.from_slice` on
## the borrowed view before it ceases to be valid.
##
## Stub: throwaway body for the existing .zag stub convention. The
## router arm REWRITES the call site before any reader sees the
## verbatim form.

pub fun getEnv(name: str) -> ?str {
    return null;
}
